    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1180-e, V & T L; amd §371-a, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7802A\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to owner\nliability for failure of operator to comply with certain posted maximum\nspeed limits in the town of Yorktown, Westchester county; and to amend\nthe general municipal law, in relation to making technical corrections\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nAllows the Department of Transportation to operate the existing highway\nwork zone speed camera program in work zones located within the Town of\nYorktown, Westchester County, upon approval of the town.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 names the act \"Jake's Law.\"\n\nSection 2 amends section 1180-e of the vehicle and traffic law to\nauthorize the commissioner of transportation to operate the existing\nhighway work zone speed camera demonstration program in highway\nconstruction and maintenance work areas located within the Town of York-\ntown, Westchester County, upon written approval from the town.  The town\nmay rescind or modify such approval at any time.\n\nSection 3 makes conforming technical amendments to section 371-a of the\ngeneral municipal law.\n\nSection 4 authorizes the Town of Yorktown and the Department of Trans-\nportation to install signage providing advance notice of photo speed\nviolation monitoring systems and authorizes the town to conduct a public\ninformation campaign regarding the program.\n\nSection 5 sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRoad work zones are dangerous areas for the men and women responsible\nfor maintaining and repairing our transportation infrastructure.  Exces-\nsive speed through active work zones places workers, motorists, and\nemergency personnel at risk of serious injury or death. Because it is\nnot practical to station law enforcement personnel at every work zone,\nautomated enforcement has become an important tool for improving compli-\nance with posted speed limits and protecting roadway workers.\n\nThis bill expands the locations where the State's existing work zone\nspeed camera program may operate by authorizing the Department of Trans-\nportation, with the approval of the Town of Yorktown, to deploy the\nprogram in work zones located within the town. By utilizing the existing\nState-administered framework, the bill will help deter dangerous speed-\ning and improve safety for workers and the traveling public.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2023-24: A.9283A\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nNinety days after it shall have become law.\n",
